+## Subtopics
+
+### Cell-tree
+A compact representation of ray dependencies between cells (flex-blocks), used for ray-task scheduling and traversal speedup. It is several orders of magnitude smaller than the full dependency list [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Moving Walls Algorithm
+The preprocessing step that partitions the scene, moving boundaries to balance load and accommodate data extents before the kd-tree is extracted [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Construction involves region growing, cropping, and kd-tree extraction. The complexity of optimal caching is NP-Complete [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The cell-tree (dependency graph) is described as being several orders of magnitude smaller than the number of dependencies it represents [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Specifically designed for distributed rendering to balance load across render nodes [S1, unofficial]. Early cropping of empty space reduces run-time for preprocessing, processing, and network movement [S1, unofficial]. Provides a deterministic depth order for image compositing in ray casting [S1, unofficial]. Accelerates ray tracing by indicating potential neighbors and automating space skipping [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Cannot guarantee single-fetch scheduling for all cells [S1, unofficial]. Requires complex preprocessing (moving walls, dynamic programming for load balancing) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Construction involves preprocessing (region growing, cropping) and tree extraction. The NP-Complete nature of optimal caching means scheduling is heuristic [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The cell-tree (dependency graph) is significantly smaller than the full set of ray dependencies, reducing memory overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Reduces end-to-end rendering time by balancing load across render nodes and minimizing network communication time [S1, unofficial]. Space skipping is automated via data extents, reducing runtime for later processing steps [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles massive volumes; load-balanced for distributed rendering; automates space skipping; works with both ray casting and ray tracing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Construction is complex (requires moving walls, DP for load balancing); optimal caching order is not guaranteed [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The flex-block tree and partition were introduced in the context of massive data management for distributed volume visualization [S1, unofficial]. The work addresses the Load Balanced Network Distribution (LBND) problem, an optimization problem for minimizing end-to-end rendering time in distributed clusters [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon traditional space subdivision methods (like kd-trees) but modifies them to address load balancing and empty space reduction specifically for distributed environments [S1, unofficial]. The approach supports both software ray tracing and hardware ray casting [S1, unofficial].
+The flex-block tree was introduced in the context of massive data management for distributed volume visualization [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the Load Balanced Network Distribution (LBND) problem, where the goal is to minimize end-to-end rendering time by balancing the slowest render node, composition time, and network communication [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to support both software ray tracing and hardware ray casting in distributed clusters [S1, unofficial].
 
